1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Here's a problem you hit the first time you call Claude in a loop. Each request waits on the

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:10,000
network and if your code blocks on each it sits doing nothing while the response travels. Async

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:17,000
and await fix that. This lesson covers non-blocking calls, the event loop, and how await frees the

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:24,000
caller. Let's put blocking and non-blocking side by side. A blocking call is the default in most

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
code. You call Claude and your program stops right there waiting for the full response before the

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
next line runs. That's fine for one request but one slow call freezes everything behind it and

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:41,000
if you have 50 to make they run strictly one after another. A non-blocking call is different.

8
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:46,000
You issue the request and await it which tells the runtime to go do other useful work

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,000
while the response is still in flight. The caller never sits idle. That single shift from waiting

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,000
to yielding is what the rest of this lesson builds on. Three building blocks make async work

11
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:04,000
so let's define them. First, the coroutine. That's a function you write with async and it can pause

12
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:10,000
part way through, hand control back, and resume later right where it stopped. Second, the event

13
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,000
loop. Think of it as a scheduler. It runs your coroutines and the instant one of them is waiting

14
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,000
on the network it advances another that's ready to go. Third, await. That's the keyword that

15
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:28,000
actually suspends a coroutine until the thing it's waiting on is ready and crucially it does that

16
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:35,000
without blocking the whole thread. Coroutine, event loop, await. Once those three click everything

17
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:42,000
else is just applying them. So why is a Claude call such a good fit for async in particular?

18
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
It comes down to where the time goes. A request to the API is IO bound which means it spends almost

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:56,000
all of its time waiting on the network not burning CPU on your machine. Picture the timeline. A tiny

20
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:02,000
sliver is your code preparing the request then a long stretch of just waiting for bytes to come

21
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:08,000
back over the wire. That waiting stretch is dead time in blocking code. Async hands it to other

22
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:14,000
work instead and it does that without spinning up extra threads. So the more calls you have in flight

23
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:21,000
the more of that idle time you reclaim and the bigger the payoff. Let's trace what await actually

24
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:27,000
does the moment you call it. You write await in front of a Claude call and right there your coroutine

25
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:33,000
pauses. Control returns to the event loop. The loop finds another coroutine that's ready to make

26
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:40,000
progress and runs that one instead. Your paused coroutine sits there costing nothing waiting for

27
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:47,000
its response. Then the instant the result lands the loop wakes your coroutine back up and it resumes

28
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:52,000
right after the await with the result in hand. Notice what's missing. Nothing is spinning in a

29
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:58,000
busy loop and nothing is polling to check if it's done. The loop wakes you when it's time not a

30
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:05,000
moment before. Two misconceptions trip people up early so let's clear both. The first is that async

31
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:12,000
means parallel. It doesn't. On a single thread async interleaves your tasks. It rapidly switches

32
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,000
between them at the await points but only one is ever actually running at a given instant.

33
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,000
The work overlaps in time but it isn't happening simultaneously on different cores.

34
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:28,000
The second misconception is that awaiting something blocks. It's actually the reverse. The

35
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:33,000
await point is precisely where your code yields. Where it steps aside so the loop can run something

36
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:40,000
else. A function with no awaits in it never gives the loop a chance to switch. So await isn't the

37
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:47,000
thing that blocks you. It's the thing that lets you not block. Let's walk through a single async call

38
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:54,000
end-to-end so the shape is concrete. Step one. You call the async version of the client method.

39
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Instead of the finished response you immediately get back a coroutine. A promise of work to be done.

40
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Step two. You await that coroutine. At that point the event loop parks your task and goes off to run

41
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:12,000
whatever else is ready. Step three. The response comes back over the network. The loop wakes your

42
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:19,000
task and your code resumes with the actual result. That's the entire cycle. One call and at no point

43
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:25,000
is a thread frozen solid or stuck in a busy wait. It just steps aside politely and picks back up when

44
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:32,000
the answer is ready. Let's separate three words people use interchangeably because the distinction

45
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:38,000
matters. Concurrency is about structure. It means you've arranged your work so many tasks are in

46
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:45,000
progress at once taking turns even if only one thread is doing the work. Parallelism is stronger.

47
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,000
It means tasks literally execute at the same instant which requires multiple cores or machines

48
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:58,000
actually running side by side. Async is one specific way to get concurrency. It uses an event

49
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:04,000
loop and a weight to interleave tasks on a single thread rather than spinning up a thread per task.

50
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:11,000
So async gives you concurrency but not necessarily parallelism. Keeping those straight explains a lot

51
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:18,000
of confusing performance behavior. Async is powerful but it's easy to accidentally undo it.

52
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,000
So let me flag the classic traps. The first is calling a blocking library from inside a coroutine.

53
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:31,000
If that call doesn't yield it stalls the entire event loop and every other task freezes along with

54
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:37,000
it. The second is forgetting to await. If you call an async function but never await the coroutine

55
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:45,000
it returns that work just quietly never runs. No error, no result. It simply sits there. The third

56
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:51,000
is doing heavy CPU work on the loop. A long computation with no awaits blocks everything

57
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:58,000
because the loop can't switch away mid-calculation. The theme across all three is the same. Keep the

58
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:04,000
event loop free to do the one thing it's great at, waiting on the network. Let's hit the bottom

59
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:11,000
line. Async lets a network-bound app issue a clog call and keep working instead of freezing on it.

60
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:17,000
It matters because most of a request is just waiting and that idle time is yours to reclaim.

61
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:25,000
Keep in mind async buys you concurrency on one thread not true parallelism. That covers async

62
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
and await for API calls. I'll see you in the next one.

